Object.assign(window.IND_FEED_BODY, {"ss-fk.karam-brothers-2":{"src":"story:fk.karam-brothers:scenes:2:text","title":"The Branch the Brothers Brought Home · 3 of 9","body":"Then came a busy year. Trade was good, the brothers were rich and rushing, and when the festival came round, they had no time. The drums played in other courtyards. In theirs, nothing — and in some tellings the sacred branch was not just forgotten but knocked aside in the hurry, which is worse.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karam-brothers.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karam-brothers|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Branch the Brothers Brought Home","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.karam-brothers-3":{"src":"story:fk.karam-brothers:scenes:3:text","title":"The Branch the Brothers Brought Home · 4 of 9","body":"And the luck left. Quietly, completely, the way a guest leaves a house where no one greets her. The rice sickened, the cattle strayed, the granary emptied, and within the year the famous brothers were poor men staring at each other across a bare table.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karam-brothers.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karam-brothers|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Branch the Brothers Brought Home","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.karam-brothers-5":{"src":"story:fk.karam-brothers:scenes:5:text","title":"The Branch the Brothers Brought Home · 6 of 9","body":"So the brothers walked. The tellings send them far — across rivers, through forests, asking everyone they met where the spirit of the karam might be found. And the search itself began to change them: two men who had had no time for anything now had time for everything, and helped whoever they met on the road, because they finally knew how it felt to need help.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karam-brothers.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karam-brothers|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Branch the Brothers Brought Home","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.karam-brothers-6":{"src":"story:fk.karam-brothers:scenes:6:text","title":"The Branch the Brothers Brought Home · 7 of 9","body":"At last, by the water, they found the karam — and they asked its forgiveness, plainly, the way you ask when you mean it. And they cut one branch with honour, and carried it home high, singing the songs their mother had taught them, every verse, the whole way back.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karam-brothers.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karam-brothers|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Branch the Brothers Brought Home","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.karam-brothers-7":{"src":"story:fk.karam-brothers:scenes:7:text","title":"The Branch the Brothers Brought Home · 8 of 9","body":"They planted the branch in their courtyard, and the household danced around it all night — and the luck came home the way the branch had: carried, honoured, sung to. The fields recovered. The brothers did not forget again. Nobody who has walked that far for a branch forgets again.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karam-brothers.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karam-brothers|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Branch the Brothers Brought Home","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bamboo-bonga-1":{"src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:scenes:1:text","title":"The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo · 2 of 9","body":"Every winter, when the forest grew lean, the elephant came up the hill to the great bamboo grove and ate. The grove always had enough: green canes at the edges, sweet shoots in the softer ground. He ate, rumbled his thanks in the general direction of nothing, and ambled off. It had worked this way his whole life.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bamboo-bonga.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bamboo-bonga-2":{"src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:scenes:2:text","title":"The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo · 3 of 9","body":"But this winter was hungrier than most, and standing in the middle of all that bamboo, the elephant had a big grey thought: why nibble at the edges? He could push the whole grove flat in an afternoon and feast for a week — the hearts of the clumps, the young shoots, everything, all at once.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bamboo-bonga.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bamboo-bonga-3":{"src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:scenes:3:text","title":"The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo · 4 of 9","body":"He had wrapped his trunk around the first great clump when the grove spoke. Not loudly. Bongas rarely need to be loud. It was a voice like wind through canes, coming from everywhere and nowhere: \"Winter guest. Before you pull — a question.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bamboo-bonga.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bamboo-bonga-5":{"src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:scenes:5:text","title":"The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo · 6 of 9","body":"\"...This grove,\" he said at last, very quietly. \"If there is one.\" He let go of the clump. It swayed back upright, and the whole grove rustled — with relief, or laughter; with bamboo it is hard to tell the difference.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bamboo-bonga.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bamboo-bonga-6":{"src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:scenes:6:text","title":"The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo · 7 of 9","body":"\"Then here is the grove's law, guest,\" said the bonga. \"Take from the edges, where I grow back. Leave the hearts, where I live. Eat your fill and leave me standing — and I will feed you, and your calves, and their calves, every winter until elephants forget the way here. That is a better bargain than one loud afternoon.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bamboo-bonga.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bamboo-bonga-7":{"src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:scenes:7:text","title":"The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo · 8 of 9","body":"The elephant ate from the edges — a very large amount, it must be said; the law says fill, and he filled. Then he touched the oldest cane gently with his trunk, the way you touch an elder's feet, and went back down the hill. And the next winter the grove was there. And the next. And the next.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bamboo-bonga.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sarhul-flowers-1":{"src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:scenes:1:text","title":"When the Sal Trees Bloom · 2 of 8","body":"It is kept, in their own ways, by many peoples of the plateau — the Oraon, the Munda, the Ho and others keep Sarhul, and the Santhals keep their own flower festival, Baha, in the same blossoming season. Different songs, different steps, one shared idea: the year begins when the trees say so.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sarhul-flowers.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from When the Sal Trees Bloom","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sarhul-flowers-2":{"src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:scenes:2:text","title":"When the Sal Trees Bloom · 3 of 8","body":"The festival goes to the sacred grove first. The village priest — the pahan — keeps the rites there on behalf of everyone, with offerings and prayers for good rains and a good year; in many villages the pahan reads the signs from vessels of water, and the village listens to what the water promises about the monsoon.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sarhul-flowers.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from When the Sal Trees Bloom","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sarhul-flowers-3":{"src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:scenes:3:text","title":"When the Sal Trees Bloom · 4 of 8","body":"Then the grove's blessing comes home. Sal blossoms are carried from the grove and set with honour on doorways and in houses — the priest and elders bringing flowers to every family, so that every roof in the village wears a piece of the forest's spring. Drums begin. Lines of dancers form. It goes on into the night, and nobody is in a hurry for it to stop.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sarhul-flowers.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from When the Sal Trees Bloom","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sarhul-flowers-5":{"src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:scenes:5:text","title":"When the Sal Trees Bloom · 6 of 8","body":"In Ranchi, Sarhul has grown into one of the great sights of the city: processions from every neighbourhood, rivers of dancers and drummers in white saris bordered with red, sal blossoms everywhere, the whole capital walking and singing. Schools close. The plateau celebrates itself.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sarhul-flowers.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from When the Sal Trees Bloom","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sarhul-flowers-6":{"src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:scenes:6:text","title":"When the Sal Trees Bloom · 7 of 8","body":"And through it all stand the trees that started it — the sal, the tree of the plateau, giver of leaf-plates and resin and shade and timber and this one week of perfume. The festival is, at its heart, a whole people turning to a forest and saying, formally and with drums: we noticed. Thank you. Again this year.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sarhul-flowers.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from When the Sal Trees Bloom","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","4 min"]]},"en-ramayana-21-1":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|21","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:cards:1:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights","body":"It was not glorious either. The vanaras went up ladders made of each other and came down again. The gates held. It rained. Rama walked the lines at night and learned the names of people who were not there the next night, and the tellings that are honest do not skip that part.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 21","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","21 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-21-2":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|21","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:cards:2:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights","body":"Indrajit was the worst of it. Ravana's eldest, and he fought from inside a cloud where nobody could see him, and on the third night he brought both brothers down at once with arrows that turned into serpents and wound them up on the ground where they lay.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 21","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","21 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-21-3":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|21","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:cards:3:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights","body":"The whole army sat down around them in the dark. That was the low point. And then something came over the sea at dawn like a wind with wings — Garuda, the great eagle, who is the enemy of every serpent there is — and every snake on that field simply let go and left.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 21","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","21 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-21-4":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|21","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:cards:4:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights","body":"Kumbhakarna came out on the fifth day, enormous and slow and sad, and he had said what he had to say to his brother already and had nothing left to say to anybody. He did a great deal of damage. When he fell, the ground jumped, and Ravana in his tower put his hands over his face and stayed like that for a long time.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 21","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","21 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-21-5":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|21","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:cards:5:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights","body":"On the seventh night Indrajit came back out of his cloud and hit Lakshmana with something nobody could stop. He went down and did not get up. Rama got to him first and could not get an answer out of him, and sat down on the ground in the middle of a battlefield holding his brother and stopped being any use to anybody at all.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 21","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","21 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-21-7":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|21","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:cards:7:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights","body":"Every head on that field turned to the same person. Hanuman was already standing up.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 21","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","21 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-21-8":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|21","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:cards:8:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights","body":"He went north over the whole subcontinent in the dark — over the bridge, over the forest, over the rivers, over the plains where his story would be told for the next three thousand years — and came down on a mountain in the snow, and looked at it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 21","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","21 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-21-9":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|21","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:cards:9:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights","body":"And the mountain was covered, absolutely covered, in small glowing plants, thousands of them, and every one of them looked exactly the same. He stood in the snow with the sky already going grey in the east, and said out loud, \"I do not know which one it is.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 21","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","21 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-21-10":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|21","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:cards:10:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights","body":"So he did not choose. He got his arms round the mountain, and pulled it out of the Himalaya, and carried the entire thing back south on one hand, and the story is told all over India of the night people looked up and saw a mountain going over their village.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 21","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","21 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-22-1":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:cards:1:text","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana","body":"\"My brother is out there in their army,\" said Ravana. \"My son is dead. Kumbhakarna is dead. What do I say to them at the end of a day like that? That I was wrong all along and I only stopped when it got expensive?\" \"Yes,\" said Mandodari. \"You say that. That is exactly what you say. It would be the bravest thing you ever did.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","22 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-22-2":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:cards:2:text","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana","body":"He stood in the doorway of his own hall for a while. Then he said, \"I cannot,\" and went out to the chariot, and that is the whole tragedy of Ravana in three words, and everything else is just how long it took.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","22 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-22-3":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:cards:3:text","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana","body":"He came out onto the field and it was quite a sight. He was magnificent. Nobody on that field had ever seen anything like him, and there is not a version of this story anywhere that pretends otherwise.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","22 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-22-4":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:cards:4:text","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana","body":"Rama was on foot. So Indra sent down a chariot for him, with a driver called Matali, and Rama said thank you to the driver and asked his name, which Matali mentioned to people for the rest of his life.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","22 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-22-5":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:cards:5:text","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana","body":"They fought all day and neither of them could finish it, and the two armies stopped fighting each other and simply stood and watched, which is a thing that has never happened in any other battle in any other story.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","22 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-22-7":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:cards:7:text","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana","body":"Lanka went absolutely silent. And then Mandodari came out of the gate and walked across that field alone to where he had fallen, and knelt down, and said, \"You were the cleverest man in the world, and you would not be told. Not by me, not by your brother, not by your own son. All this. For one thing you could have undone in a morning.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","22 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-22-8":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:cards:8:text","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana","body":"Vibhishana would not do the funeral rites at first. He said the man had thrown him out of his own house. Rama said: \"He is not your enemy any more. That ended this evening. He is your brother, and he was a great king, and you will do it properly, and I will stand there while you do.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","22 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-22-9":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:cards:9:text","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana","body":"And in some tellings — this is not in Valmiki, but it is told in a good many homes and it is loved — Rama sends Lakshmana across the field to sit at Ravana's head and ask him for whatever he has left to teach, because there was more learning in that man than anyone alive, and it would be a waste to let it go into the ground.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","22 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-22-10":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|22","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:cards:10:text","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana","body":"The war was over. It had taken most of a month. The vanaras did not cheer for very long, because they were counting who was left, and a great many of them were not.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 22","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","22 of 24"]]},"en-mahabharata-14-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|14","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"\"Go and ask my husband this. When he staked me — had he already lost himself? Because if he had, then he was not his own man. And a man who does not own himself cannot stake anybody else. So ask the hall: whose was I, at that moment?\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","14 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-14-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|14","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"The messenger carried it back and said it out loud, and the hall had no answer. Yudhishthira sat and looked at the floor and did not speak. He never answers it. Not that day, not in the forest, not in thirteen years.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","14 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-14-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|14","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"So Duryodhana sent his brother Duhshasana to bring her by force, and he did, and she was dragged into the hall in front of her husbands and her elders and everybody in the kingdom who mattered.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","14 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-14-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|14","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"What happened next in that hall is the reason people still say the name of this family with their jaw set. We are not going to describe it. In many tellings something stops it; the manuscripts do not all tell it the same way. What every version agrees on is what the room did, which is nothing.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","14 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-14-5":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|14","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:5:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"She asked her question again, standing up, to the whole hall. And she asked it of the one man there whose whole life was made of keeping his word.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","14 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-14-6":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|14","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:6:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"\"The law about who owns what is subtle,\" said Bhishma slowly, \"and I cannot see the bottom of it. I do not know the answer to your question.\" An old man who had never once been unsure of anything, saying that, in that room.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","14 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-14-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|14","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"And it was Karna who shouted him down. Karna, who had been turned away at a gate himself, said the cruellest thing said in that hall all day, and he said it to her. The most sympathetic person in this poem is on the wrong side of this room, doing the worst thing he ever does.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","14 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-14-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|14","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"Bhima made an oath about Duhshasana's hands so violent that the court flinched. Draupadi untied her hair and said it would stay untied until she had a reason to bind it up again, and everybody there understood exactly what she meant.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","14 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-14-10":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|14","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:10:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"And then jackals started calling in the courtyard in broad daylight, and the old blind king, who had let all of it happen because it was his son doing it, got frightened.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","14 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-14-11":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|14","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:11:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"\"Ask me for anything,\" said Dhritarashtra. She asked for her husbands' freedom. \"Ask again.\" She asked for their weapons and their chariots back. \"Ask a third time.\" And she said no.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","14 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-14-12":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|14","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:12:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"\"I have asked twice, and a person may ask twice,\" she said. \"Whatever else we need, my husbands can fetch for themselves.\" She got it. She walked out of that hall with everything she had come to get, and the question still lying on the floor.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","14 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-15-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|15","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw","body":"Then Duryodhana went to his father, with Shakuni behind him, and said what people say when they are winning and have been made to stop: \"You gave it all back. In one afternoon you gave away everything I won. Do you know what they will do with it?\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","15 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-15-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|15","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw","body":"Gandhari came and stood in that room, with the cloth over her eyes, and said the only completely sensible sentence anybody says in the entire Sabha Parva.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","15 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-15-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|15","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw","body":"\"Send him away,\" she said. \"Today. He is my son and I have carried him and I am telling you he is going to burn this house down with all of us inside it. You can still do it this morning. You will not be able to do it next year.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","15 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-15-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|15","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw","body":"\"I cannot,\" said Dhritarashtra. \"He is my son.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","15 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-15-5":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|15","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:cards:5:text","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw","body":"That is the whole tragedy in six words, and every parent who reads it feels the floor go. He was not a wicked man. He was a man who could never once say no to his child, and the price of that was counted out at the end in bodies.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","15 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-15-7":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|15","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw","body":"The loser would go into the forest for twelve years. And then a thirteenth year, living somewhere in the world in disguise — and if anybody recognised them in that thirteenth year, the whole thing started again from the beginning.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","15 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-15-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|15","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw","body":"Yudhishthira sat down and picked the dice up. Again. Knowing exactly who he was playing, exactly how it had gone the day before, and exactly what it had already cost his wife.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","15 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-15-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|15","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw","body":"He lost.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","15 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-15-10":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|15","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:cards:10:text","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw","body":"They changed into bark and deerskin in the courtyard and walked out through the city they had grown up in, and the people of Hastinapura came out and lined the road and cried, and nobody in the palace came to the gate.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","15 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-16-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|16","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest","body":"The argument is this. Draupadi and Bhima want to go back and take the kingdom now. Yudhishthira gave his word for thirteen years and will not move. And Arjuna, mostly, sits between them and says nothing, which does not help.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","16 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-16-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|16","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest","body":"\"You are not even angry,\" Draupadi said to him one night. \"That is the part I cannot forgive. I was dragged into a hall. You sat and looked at the floor. And now we are in a wood eating roots and you are calm.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","16 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-16-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|16","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest","body":"\"I am not asking you to be a good man,\" she said. \"You are a good man, everybody knows, it is the only thing anyone ever says about you. I am asking you to be angry for one hour. Just one.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","16 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-16-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|16","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest","body":"\"If I am good only when it pays,\" said Yudhishthira, \"then I was never good, I was only lucky. I gave my word for thirteen years. I am not going to spend thirteen years explaining to people why my word does not count.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","16 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-16-5":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|16","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:cards:5:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest","body":"\"Thirteen years,\" said Bhima, poking the fire. \"I could do the whole thing in an afternoon. Give me the afternoon.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","16 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-16-7":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|16","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest","body":"One thing did happen. Draupadi wanted a particular flower she had seen come down a stream, and Bhima went off up the mountain to find where it grew, shouting and knocking trees over the way he did.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","16 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-16-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|16","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest","body":"An old monkey was lying across the path with its tail stretched right over it, and would not move. \"Step over me,\" it said. \"I am old.\" \"I will not step over you, that is rude. Move it.\" \"Move it yourself, boy. I am tired.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","16 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-16-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|16","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest","body":"Bhima bent down to flick the tail aside with one hand, and could not shift it. Then both hands. Then everything he had. And the old monkey watched him with great interest and said, \"Yes. It is like that sometimes.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","16 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-16-10":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|16","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:cards:10:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest","body":"Bhima stopped and looked at him properly and went down on the path. \"You are Hanuman.\" \"And you are my brother,\" said Hanuman. \"Sons of the wind, both of us. Go and get the flower. And when your day comes, look up at your brother's chariot. I will be on the flag.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","16 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-17-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|17","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain","body":"Then a wild boar came crashing through the scrub straight at him, and he had a bow in his hands before he had finished thinking about it, and he shot.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","17 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-17-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|17","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain","body":"So did somebody else. Two arrows, one boar, the same instant. And out of the trees came a huge hunter with a bow, in skins, with a woman walking behind him, entirely at home up there where nobody lives.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","17 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-17-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|17","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain","body":"\"My kill,\" said the hunter cheerfully. \"I saw it first.\" \"You did not,\" said Arjuna, who had been standing on one leg for weeks and was not in a reasonable frame of mind. \"It came at me. It is mine.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","17 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-17-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|17","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain","body":"They argued about a pig on a mountainside like two boys at a village fair. Then they shot at each other, and then they wrestled, and Arjuna — the finest archer alive, the one who only ever sees the eye — lost.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","17 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-17-5":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|17","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:cards:5:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain","body":"He lost his arrows, which came back empty. He lost his bow. He lost the fight with his hands. He ended up flat on the stones with nothing left, which had never once happened to him in his life.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","17 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-17-7":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|17","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain","body":"And when he looked up, the flowers were sitting on the hunter's head.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","17 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-17-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|17","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain","body":"He got up very slowly. \"You,\" he said. And Shiva laughed at him, standing there in his skins with the flowers on, and said, \"You fought me for an hour and you were not bad.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","17 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-17-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|17","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain","body":"\"Nobody comes up here to be told they are wonderful,\" said Shiva. \"You came up to be beaten. Now you have been, so you can be given something.\" And he gave him the weapon that Arjuna would carry all the way to the last day and, in the end, would not use.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","17 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-17-10":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|17","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:cards:10:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain","body":"Arjuna stayed away five years in all. He learned things, and he was taught to dance and to sing by the musicians of the sky, which sounds like a joke and turns out — exactly one book later — to be the thing that saves all five of them.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","17 of 33"]]}});
